Question Four: Will Robots Take Over Physical Work?

The Optimist

The Optimist points to rapid advances in machine vision, force sensing, dexterous manipulation and systems that connect language instructions to physical actions.

Traditional industrial robots worked well because the environment was built around them. Parts arrived in predictable positions. Movements were repeated. People remained outside safety cages.

The new ambition is different.

A general-purpose robot would perceive an unfamiliar environment, interpret an instruction, select a tool and adapt its movement as conditions changed.

The progression is:

\text{fixed automation}
\rightarrow
\text{adaptive automation}
\rightarrow
\text{general physical agents}

Humanoid machines are attractive because the existing world was designed around human dimensions. Stairs, doors, ladders, handles, controls, workbenches and tools all assume an approximately human body.

Rather than rebuilding every workplace for specialised machinery, the Optimist expects adaptable robots to enter the human environment directly.

The early machines will be expensive and limited. Repetition, fleet data and improved hardware will gradually expand the range of tasks they can perform.

Physical labour will not disappear in one event.

It will be absorbed operation by operation.

The Pessimist

The Pessimist sees the same progress and focuses on the consequences of failure.

A language model generating a wrong paragraph produces bad text.

A heavy robot generating a wrong movement near a person produces an industrial incident.

Physical systems multiply the consequences of software error because the output is no longer symbolic. It contains mass, speed, heat, pressure and stored energy.

The robot also creates new attack surfaces:

* cameras can be deceived or obscured;
* sensors can become contaminated;
* communications can be interrupted;
* software updates can introduce faults;
* actuators can wear;
* and remote systems can be compromised.

As societies become dependent on robotic labour, failures in chips, batteries, networks or specialised components may propagate into food, logistics, manufacturing and infrastructure maintenance.

The Pessimist therefore sees a second danger beyond the individual machine.

A highly automated society may become more productive during normal operation while becoming less capable of surviving abnormal operation.

If the people who once performed the work no longer exist in sufficient numbers, a widespread system failure becomes more than an inconvenience.

It becomes a competence crisis.

The Sceptic

The Sceptic separates demonstrations from sustained operation.

A robot performing a difficult task once is evidence that the task can be performed.

It is not evidence that the machine can perform the task thousands of times, across changing environments, at an economically acceptable failure rate, without continuous human preparation and recovery.

Reliability compounds across long sequences.

Suppose a physical process requires 100 dependent actions, and the robot succeeds at each one with a probability of 0.99. Under the simplified assumption that the errors are independent, the probability of completing the entire sequence successfully is:

0.99^{100}
\approx
0.366

A 99 per cent success rate at the level of individual actions can therefore produce only a 36.6 per cent success rate for the complete job.

Physical work also contains asymmetric failure costs.

A robot that moves 99 drums correctly but drops a hazardous one on the hundredth attempt is not necessarily a commercially successful system with a 99 per cent score.

The cost of one failure may exceed the value of all the successful operations.

The Sceptic does not deny that robotics will improve.

He asks whether the relevant evidence concerns the impressive movement shown in the demonstration or the complete maintenance, safety and recovery cycle required in an ordinary workplace.

Dave

Dave has watched automated polishing systems work in an environment that is wet, abrasive, noisy and permanently trying to contaminate itself.

The machine may be capable of applying a controlled sequence of pressures and abrasive stages across metres of stone.

The sales presentation shows the clean movement.

The factory experiences the water.

Slurry circulates through the system. Fine particles reach places they were never invited to enter. Nozzles begin to obstruct. Seals wear. Heads stick. Bearings alter their sound before a dashboard necessarily declares a failure.

The robot may continue moving within its programmed tolerances while applying the wrong physical condition to an expensive component.

The machine has automated the motion.

It has not automated the ecology surrounding the motion.

Dave’s robotics model is:

\text{useful robot}
=
\text{movement}
+
\text{perception}
+
\text{safety}
+
\text{maintenance}
+
\text{recovery}

Most demonstrations emphasise the first two terms.

Dave spends his time in the last three.

He also knows that the safety-critical part of a robot cannot depend entirely on a distant server. Remote systems may train models, distribute updates and support planning. Immediate braking, stability and entry into a safe state must remain local.

His version is simple:

“The cloud can be the university. It can’t be the spinal cord.”

And when somebody tells him that a robot will soon maintain the whole plant, Dave does not ask whether it can recognise a pump.

He asks whether it can isolate the system, reach the pump, remove the obstruction, deal with whatever has leaked around it, find the correct replacement, fit it in a space designed for human arms and restart the line without creating a second fault.

A robot may eventually perform every step.

Dave’s point is that demonstrating one step does not close the sequence.

“Everyone wants to talk about machine vision. Nobody wants to talk about dirty water.”

