Question Five: Will AI Make Everyone Rich?

The Optimist

The Optimist begins with productivity.

If intelligence becomes cheaper, many activities become cheaper with it. Software can be produced more quickly. Designs can be tested before physical manufacture. Administrative work can be reduced. Medical and educational expertise can be distributed more widely.

The central relationship is:

\text{greater output per unit input}
\rightarrow
\text{greater total wealth}

AI may allow one engineer to perform work that previously required a team. It may allow a small business to access planning, analysis and software development that was once available only to large firms.

As productive capacity rises, competition should reduce prices. Goods and services that currently contain substantial cognitive labour may become widely accessible.

The Optimist does not claim that every price falls immediately. Land, energy and materials remain physical constraints.

The argument is that intelligence helps society use those constraints more efficiently and discover new ways around them.

Cheap expertise may not produce literal post-scarcity.

It can still produce an unprecedented expansion in what an ordinary person can create, understand and access.

The Pessimist

The Pessimist asks who owns the productive system.

Value creation and value distribution are different processes:

\text{value created}
\neq
\text{value captured}
\neq
\text{value distributed}

If AI allows a company to produce the same output with fewer workers, the saving initially belongs to the company.

It may be passed to customers through lower prices.

It may be paid to workers through higher wages.

It may instead become profit, executive compensation, market power or further investment in automation.

The outcome depends on ownership and bargaining power, not on technical capability alone.

If the most capable models, data centres and energy infrastructure remain concentrated, the owners of those assets may receive a growing share of the economic return.

Workers then compete for the smaller collection of tasks the machinery has not absorbed.

The Pessimist therefore sees a future in which society becomes wealthier in aggregate while many individuals become less economically secure.

Abundance in production does not guarantee abundance in income.

The Sceptic

The Sceptic asks which costs AI can actually reduce.

Digital goods have unusual economics. Once a piece of software exists, another copy can be produced at very low marginal cost.

Housing, energy, food, care, land and transport do not behave in the same way.

An AI can produce a building design rapidly.

It cannot remove the cost of land, planning approval, concrete, steel, labour, financing and grid connection merely by making the drawing cheaper.

A more capable optimisation system may reduce waste in those processes. It does not dissolve their physical scarcity.

In some cases, cheaper capability may also increase total consumption. If generating software, images or analysis becomes inexpensive, people may request far more of it, increasing demand for compute and energy.

The Sceptic therefore expects uneven abundance.

Some forms of knowledge work may become dramatically cheaper.

Other parts of life may remain expensive because the bottleneck was never the availability of text, software or advice.

Dave

Dave watched a major customer respond to problems in its supply chain by bringing more of the production process under its own control.

The customer did not reject automation.

It built its own automated capability.

That distinction matters.

The technology may have reduced the labour required for selected operations, but the commercial value did not distribute itself evenly across the old supply chain.

The company that owned the customer relationship, the production facility, the scheduling decisions and the quality feedback was in the strongest position to capture the benefit.

From Dave’s perspective:

\text{technical saving}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{shared saving}

The machine manufacturer sells the equipment.

The model provider may sell the intelligence.

The worker supplies the remaining judgement and labour.

But the company controlling the order, the product and the customer often decides where the margin remains.

Dave also knows that installing expensive automation does not itself create profit. A plant can contain cutting-edge machinery and still lose work, lose control of quality or fail as a business.

The machine creates productive options.

It does not supply customers, pricing power, working capital or competent management.

Dave’s answer is:

“The technology can make someone rich. That doesn’t tell you who.”

If the same company owns the model, the machinery, the product and the customer, it may capture a large share of the value.

If those layers are split across suppliers, contractors and workers, the savings will be distributed according to contracts and market power.

Not according to who performed the most difficult work.

