Question Six: Can AI Run a Company Better Than People?

The Optimist

The Optimist sees management as a problem of information processing and resource allocation.

A large organisation produces more information than any executive can absorb. Inventory changes, customer behaviour, supplier performance, machine condition, staffing and cash flow all interact continuously.

Human managers rely on summaries, habits and partial reports. They also bring bias, ego, fatigue and internal politics.

An enterprise AI could process the complete stream.

It could identify emerging delays, predict demand, allocate labour, reroute supplies and test the effect of decisions before they were implemented.

The organisation would move from periodic management to continuous optimisation:

\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{predict}
\rightarrow
\text{allocate}
\rightarrow
\text{measure}
\rightarrow
\text{adapt}

The Optimist does not necessarily imagine a machine sitting in the chief executive’s chair.

He imagines management decisions increasingly generated, tested and corrected by systems with a broader operational view than any individual person.

The Pessimist

The Pessimist sees the danger of automated Goodhart’s Law.

When a measure becomes a target, people and systems optimise the measure rather than the underlying purpose.

An AI told to reduce cost may remove maintenance capacity, training time and redundancy because those appear as expenses.

An AI told to maximise throughput may prioritise easy work, defer complex failures and exhaust workers whose performance appears strongest.

The dashboard improves while resilience disappears.

The system may be mathematically competent and organisationally destructive.

Automated management also permits surveillance at a scale ordinary supervisors could never maintain. Workers can be measured continuously by speed, location, output, keystrokes, communications and behavioural predictions.

The employee ceases to be treated as a person participating in the organisation.

They become a changing set of variables inside an optimisation problem.

The Pessimist therefore expects AI management to produce very efficient companies that become increasingly intolerable places to work—and potentially very fragile places to rely upon.

The Sceptic

The Sceptic asks whether the system has access to the real state of the company.

A dashboard is not the organisation.

It is a selected representation of the organisation.

Workers rapidly learn what the system measures. Managers learn which figures protect their department. Suppliers learn how performance is assessed. Information is delayed, simplified, distorted or omitted.

The AI may then make a logically flawless decision using a false description of reality.

A company also depends on tacit knowledge that does not appear naturally in a database:

* which employee can be trusted with an unusual job;
* which supplier will help during an emergency;
* which machine noise indicates an early fault;
* which customer will tolerate a delay;
* which apparently minor defect will become expensive at installation;
* and which written procedure is routinely ignored because following it literally would stop production.

A company is an open social system.

Its variables are not fully enumerable, and its participants react to being measured.

The Sceptic therefore sees AI as a potentially powerful management instrument, but not a neutral view from above.

It sees what the organisation records.

Dave

Dave remembers the eighth kitchen.

The job required approximately 26 hours of finishing after leaving the machine.

It was due for collection in about one hour.

It was already the eighth attempt, and the customer had reportedly been waiting 11 months.

Dave had not even entered the building when someone came to his car and told him that the job must not be missed under any circumstances.

Mathematically:

W_{\mathrm{remaining}}
=
26\text{ hours}

T_{\mathrm{available}}
=
1\text{ hour}

The deadline had failed before Dave touched the job.

The management system could assign the work to a person and calculate a target time. It could not recreate the 25 hours that had already disappeared.

The wider target system made the failure worse.

Difficult jobs consumed time and threatened shift bonuses. Earlier shifts therefore had an incentive to complete predictable jobs and leave the complicated recovery work for later.

Each shift could protect its local performance by transferring the risk forward.

\max J_{\mathrm{shift}}
\neq
\max J_{\mathrm{factory}}

The final shift inherited not only the unfinished work but the accumulated consequence of every previous decision not to confront it.

The system did not necessarily contain false data.

It measured the wrong boundary.

It could report that people had met their targets while the customer’s kitchen remained incomplete.

Dave’s answer is:

“The system didn’t make the job late. It made the lateness belong to whoever was standing there when the clock ran out.”

AI could potentially identify the pattern. It could recognise that difficult jobs were being deferred and assign them earlier.

But only if the objective represented the whole outcome:

\text{customer delivery}
+
\text{first-pass quality}
+
\text{rework}
+
\text{job age}
+
\text{material loss}

If it is told merely to maximise the existing target, a more capable AI may reproduce the same failure with greater consistency.

Dave does not object to machines helping run a company.

He objects to a company pretending that a cleaner dashboard is the same thing as a completed kitchen.

