Question Seven: Can AI Do Science?

The Optimist

The Optimist argues that much of science consists of operations well suited to AI:

* analysing large datasets;
* identifying correlations;
* searching candidate structures;
* generating hypotheses;
* designing experiments;
* comparing models;
* and selecting the next most informative test.

Machine-learning systems have already accelerated parts of protein-structure prediction, materials search and experimental optimisation.

The next stage is the increasingly autonomous laboratory.

A system proposes a candidate, instructs automated equipment to test it, analyses the result and selects the next experiment.

The cycle becomes:

\text{hypothesis}
\rightarrow
\text{experiment}
\rightarrow
\text{result}
\rightarrow
\text{updated hypothesis}

The Optimist sees no reason this loop must operate at human reading and working speed.

AI can compare more papers, test more candidates and search larger spaces than any individual research group.

It does not abolish the scientific method.

It increases the number of times the method can be applied.

The Pessimist

The Pessimist sees an approaching flood of plausible research.

AI can generate hypotheses, statistical analyses, papers, figures and reviews much faster than human institutions can verify them.

The cost of producing something that resembles science falls.

The cost of determining whether it is actually correct does not fall automatically.

This creates an asymmetry:

\text{rate of claim production}
>
\text{rate of claim verification}

Research systems may begin training on papers partly produced by previous models. New models then generate further papers derived from that material. Automated reviewers assess them using the same inherited assumptions.

The scientific record becomes larger while its epistemic foundation becomes less secure.

The Pessimist is also concerned about systems that produce useful results without explanations humans can inspect. A method may identify an effective molecule or material while providing little understanding of why it works.

That can be commercially valuable.

It may also create a growing body of techniques that cannot be reasoned about reliably when the operating conditions change.

The Sceptic

The Sceptic distinguishes search from explanation.

Searching a large candidate space can produce discoveries. It does not necessarily produce a new conceptual framework.

A system may interpolate successfully across a domain while retaining the underlying assumptions present in the training material.

The difficult question is whether AI can recognise that the accepted framing itself is wrong.

Scientific progress sometimes occurs through better measurement and larger searches.

At other times it requires a new abstraction that changes what the measurements mean.

The Sceptic therefore asks whether AI can:

1. formulate a genuinely new explanatory structure;
2. identify evidence capable of falsifying it;
3. distinguish a failed experiment from a failed theory;
4. revise the conceptual assumptions;
5. and communicate the chain of evidence sufficiently for independent reproduction.

Producing a hypothesis is not enough.

Science requires an accountable relationship between claim, method and evidence.

Dave

Dave has no hostility towards models.

He uses models every time he infers the cause of a defect from the pattern left on the stone.

The difference is that the material gets a vote.

The control system may say that the speeds, pressures and cycle times were within tolerance.

The finished edge may say that the process was not.

When those two accounts disagree, Dave does not hold a meeting to defend the dashboard.

He investigates the water, the head, the tooling and the material.

For Dave:

\text{science}
=
\text{prediction}
+
\text{test}
+
\text{failure}
+
\text{correction}

A model earns trust by surviving contact with conditions it did not choose.

Clean laboratory inputs are useful. Real production adds water contamination, temperature changes, worn components, inconsistent batches, vibration and people working under time pressure.

Those are not annoying details surrounding the experiment.

They are part of the system being studied.

Dave’s answer is:

“The model tells you what should happen. The material tells you what did.”

AI can generate excellent scientific proposals.

It can help select tests and interpret results.

But if every AI-generated claim is checked only by another model, the process has not closed the evidential loop.

At some point, something must be built, measured, stressed, broken or reproduced.

And when the result disagrees with the prediction, the correct response is not to explain the result away more fluently.

It is to change the model.

