Question Ten: Where Does AI Actually Go?

The Optimist’s Forecast: The Abundance Trajectory

AI capability continues improving across language, software, scientific reasoning and physical systems.

The cost of useful cognitive work falls. Individuals gain access to expertise that once required large organisations. Small teams build products and conduct research at a scale previously unavailable to them.

Scientific discovery accelerates as models search larger spaces, design better experiments and connect knowledge across disciplines.

Robotics develops more slowly than software but eventually expands from controlled industrial environments into construction, logistics, agriculture, maintenance and care.

Economic institutions adapt. New mechanisms distribute part of the productivity gain. Necessary labour falls, and a growing share of human time moves towards creativity, relationships, exploration and self-directed work.

The future does not become effortless.

It becomes less constrained by the scarcity of intelligence.

AI remains a tool, but one so widely available that it substantially expands individual agency.

The Pessimist’s Forecast: The Asymmetric Panopticon

Capability improves rapidly, but ownership remains concentrated.

A small number of companies and states control the largest systems, the infrastructure beneath them and the platforms through which people work and communicate.

AI weakens labour bargaining power faster than public institutions can respond. Productivity rises while wages, security and autonomy fail to follow.

Automated systems increasingly decide who receives work, credit, insurance, education, medical attention and political visibility.

The technology is used less to liberate people than to measure, predict and manage them.

The economy becomes more efficient and more unequal.

Critical institutions depend on systems too complex to audit and too commercially valuable to interrupt.

Society does not lose control because one machine declares itself ruler.

It loses control because millions of decisions are transferred gradually into systems owned by actors who cannot be challenged meaningfully.

The Sceptic’s Forecast: Plateau and Integration

The most dramatic extrapolations fail.

Capability continues improving, but the gains become harder and more expensive. Data, energy, reliability and deployment costs slow the apparent exponential curve.

AI becomes a general component of software rather than an independent civilisation.

It is integrated into search, design tools, diagnostics, manufacturing systems, education, administration and scientific workflows.

Some occupations change substantially. Some disappear. Others remain because the cost of replacing the complete human role exceeds the value of automating it.

The hype cycle fades.

AI becomes normal infrastructure: important, useful and sometimes dangerous, but constrained by economics, regulation and the persistent difficulty of making open systems behave predictably.

The future looks different.

It does not become unrecognisable.

Dave’s Forecast: Tuesday Morning

The software keeps improving.

Reports that took days appear in seconds. Maintenance manuals become easier to search. Production schedules become more responsive. Fault histories can be compared across thousands of machines.

Some tasks genuinely disappear.

Some jobs require fewer people.

A well-designed AI system helps Dave identify a problem earlier, find the correct part and stop the same failure recurring.

A badly designed one creates another dashboard.

It generates alerts that somebody must interpret, assigns exact times to jobs whose physical condition it cannot see and produces an efficiency score that rewards everyone for avoiding the work most likely to destroy the customer relationship.

Companies recruit fewer beginners because the machine performs the simple work.

Years later, they discover that the experienced people are retiring and nobody else knows why the fourth head is ruining the finish.

The robot becomes more capable.

It still requires local safety, clean water, maintained bearings, spare parts and physical access to the thing that has failed.

The model provider sells intelligence.

The manufacturer owns the machine.

The retailer owns the customer.

The worker owns the consequences until somebody writes a contract saying otherwise.

At 07:00 on a freezing Tuesday morning, the system reports that production is operating at 98 per cent efficiency.

Dave is standing in the bay with slurry on his boot and a spanner in his hand, trying to bleed the air from a line before the courier arrives.

His forecast is not that AI fails.

It is that AI becomes extremely useful while remaining embedded inside systems that are physical, political, commercial and human.

The future contains more intelligence.

It also contains blocked pipes.

